        <div id="specs-that-matter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Shop Vac Specs Actually Matter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Airflow, sealed suction and hose diameter matter; peak horsepower mostly does not. The rest of this section explains why, and which practical features separate a vac you enjoy using from one that stays in the corner.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is Peak Horsepower Worth Comparing?</h3>

          <p>
            Hardly. Peak horsepower usually gets the largest print on the box, yet it describes what the motor puts out for an instant with no load on it, a condition you never meet while cleaning. A fan, housing and hose that differ between two models can make vacs with identical peak HP ratings perform nothing alike. Use the number as a loose indication of size class and nothing more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Do CFM and Water Lift Tell You?</h3>

          <p>
            Two other figures are far more revealing. Airflow, quoted in CFM, measures the volume of air the vac moves, and a lot of it is what lifts light, bulky material such as sawdust, leaves and drywall dust. Sealed suction, sometimes called water lift and quoted in inches, measures how hard the motor pulls against a blocked opening; plenty of it helps with heavy debris, wet pickup and long hose runs. When a brand publishes both, you finally have something solid to compare.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Numbers Worth Checking</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2.5 in.</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Hose that swallows chips and debris</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">100+ CFM</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Airflow for everyday cleanup</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50+ in.</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Water lift for liquids</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">9-16 gal</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Usual garage tank range</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read those targets as rules of thumb for mid-size corded vacs rather than hard cut-offs. Plenty of manufacturers quote CFM measured at the motor instead of at the end of the hose, which makes the figure look better than what reaches the nozzle. Even so, a vac with a modest peak HP rating, strong published airflow and a wide hose will usually beat one with a larger horsepower claim and a skinny hose.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Which Features Make a Vac Easier to Live With?</h3>

          <p>
            Performance is only half the story. Look for a drain port for emptying water, casters that roll over extension cords instead of snagging on them, somewhere on the body to wrap the hose and cord, a blower port, and filters and bags that a nearby store actually stocks. If replacement filters are a hassle to find, you end up running a clogged one, and a vac with a clogged filter is a vac that barely works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Size Hose Should a Shop Vac Have?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For garage and workshop debris, choose a 2-1/2-inch hose; it is the most useful spec on the whole machine. That width lets wood chips, small drywall offcuts, screws, leaves and gravel through without snagging. A 1.875-inch hose copes with most dust and small debris but clogs more often on bulky material. The 1.25-inch hose found on many compact vacs is meant for cars and small spills, and it jams quickly on anything coarser than coarse sawdust.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why Does a Narrow Hose Feel Stronger?</h3>

          <p>
            A wide hose carries more air at a lower speed, while a narrow one carries less air at a higher speed. That is why a slim hose feels punchy at the nozzle and works well for pulling dust out of crevices and car upholstery, while a wide hose wins on volume. Plenty of woodworkers own both: the wide hose for sweeping the floor, and a narrower hose or an adapter for hooking up to tool dust ports.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
