          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Do Filters and Add-Ons Cost?</h3>

          <p>
            Set money aside for consumables. A fine-dust or HEPA cartridge can cost $25 to $60, and bags are a few dollars apiece. A cyclone separator runs $30 to $100, plus a bucket. All of it is money well spent: these parts lengthen motor and filter life and make the vac much better on fine dust.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where Is the Best Value?</h3>

          <p>
            For most homeowners, a 12 to 14-gallon vac with a 2-1/2-inch hose, a drain port and plenty of filter options strikes the best balance between capability and cost. Add a compact or cordless vac if you need something portable, and step up to an extractor only if you sand indoors often. If a planer is also on your shopping list, read the dust collection advice in our <a href="/best-benchtop-planer/">benchtop planer guide</a> before you decide.
          </p>
