        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Common Mistakes With Shop Vacs
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Paper Filter In for Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A paper cartridge soaks up water, sags and turns mildewy, and once it is waterlogged it chokes the airflow so the motor strains. Take the dry filter off before any wet job and fit a foam sleeve or the wet filter named in the manual. Dry the tank and cage fully before the paper filter goes back.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Drywall Dust Without a Bag</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drywall and concrete dust slip through ordinary filters and settle all over the room, and whatever the filter does catch blinds it within minutes. Run a collection bag with a fine-dust or HEPA filter for these jobs. The bag traps most of the dust, holds suction steady and makes emptying much cleaner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shopping by Gallons, Not Hose Diameter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A large tank behind a narrow hose jams on chips and leaves that a smaller vac with a wide hose clears without trouble. In a garage or workshop, a 2-1/2-inch hose counts for more than extra capacity. Settle on the hose first, then choose the smallest tank that handles your water and debris.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sucking Up Hot Ash or Flammable Liquids</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Embers can smolder inside the tank for hours and set a fire long after the vac is stored. Gasoline, solvents and thinner release vapors the motor can ignite. Wait until ashes are completely cold and use a metal ash vac rated for them, and soak up flammable liquids with absorbent material instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Filter Until Suction Dies</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dust-caked filter can slash airflow, and owners frequently blame the motor for it. Knock the filter clean after dusty jobs, keep a spare ready to swap in, and replace any cartridge that is torn or water-damaged. Keeping the filter clean is the cheapest way to make a shop vac perform like new.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which shop vac is best for a typical garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 12 to 14-gallon vac with a 2-1/2-inch hose and a drain port suits most garages and home workshops, and the Ridgid HD1400 NXT matches that profile closely. If flooding or renovation debris is a regular problem, the extra capacity of the Craftsman 16-gallon earns its keep. For cars and small spills, a compact 5 or 6-gallon vac is easier to handle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is peak horsepower a useful way to compare shop vacs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not really. Peak horsepower describes motor output for an instant with no load, which never happens during actual vacuuming. Airflow in CFM, sealed suction in inches of water lift and hose diameter tell you much more. Two vacs carrying the same peak HP rating can perform very differently because of their fan, housing and hose design.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What hose diameter should I choose?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pick a 2-1/2-inch hose for garage and workshop debris, since it passes chips, leaves and screws without jamming. A 1.875-inch hose suits general dust and hooking up to tools, while a 1.25-inch hose is meant for cars and small jobs. Many owners keep the wide hose for floors plus an adapter or second hose for tools and tight spots.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is a HEPA filter actually necessary?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a HEPA-rated filter together with a collection bag whenever you clean up drywall dust, concrete or masonry dust, or fine sanding dust. Concrete and masonry dust contain silica, and OSHA construction guidance calls for HEPA vacuums in many cleanup tasks. For ordinary debris, sawdust and water, a standard filter does the job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a shop vac pick up water from a flooded basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, if the water is shallow. Take out the paper filter, fit a foam sleeve, plug into a GFCI outlet and keep the cord connection dry, then empty through the drain port when the float cuts the airflow. If the flooding is deep or the water is near outlets and appliances, shut the power off at the panel and call a professional before going in.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What usually causes a sudden drop in suction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In order of likelihood: a clogged filter, a full tank that has closed the float, a blockage in the hose or nozzle, a loose lid or missing gasket, and a damaged hose. Remove the hose and check suction at the tank inlet to narrow things down. The motor can fail, but that is far less common than a clogged filter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a dust extractor instead of a shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only when you regularly sand, rout or cut indoors and want the dust caught at the tool. Extractors run quieter and filter better, but they cost several times as much and use smaller hoses that are poor at floor debris. For garage cleanup, water and the occasional pile of sawdust, a shop vac with a good filter and a bag is better value.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

