        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The Ridgid HD1400 NXT is the right shop vac for most garages and home workshops, thanks to its 2-1/2-inch hose, drain port, manageable 14-gallon tank and filters sold nearly everywhere. Step up to the Craftsman 16-gallon for regular water and renovation cleanup, take the Vacmaster Beast if a shop blower would earn its keep, and keep a compact DeWalt or Shop-Vac for the car and small messes. Anyone sanding indoors should give a dust extractor such as the Fein Turbo II serious thought.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever you choose, judge it on hose diameter and airflow instead of peak horsepower, match the filter to the dust you make, and add a bag or a cyclone separator for fine material. Remove the paper filter before vacuuming water, never let flammables or hot ash into the tank, and clean the filter often.
